ocalization of translation initiation factor IF2</article-title><source>RNA</source><year>2003</year><volume>9</volume><fpage>958</fpage><lpage>969</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12869707</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b24"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Merrick</surname><given-names>WC</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Mechanism and regulation of eukaryotic protein synthesis</article-title><source>Microbiol Rev</source><year>1992</year><volume>56</volume><fpage>291</fpage><lpage>315</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">1620067</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b25"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Ohsawa</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Chemical modification in situ of Escherichia coli 30 S ribosomal proteins by the site-specific reagent pyridoxal phosphate. Inactivation of the aminoacyl-tRNA and mRNA binding sites</article-title><source>J Biol Chem</source><year>1983</year><volume>258</volume><fpage>150</fpage><lpage>156</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">6336745</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b26"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Parmeggiani</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Properties and regulation of the GTPase activities of elongation factors Tu and G, and of initiation factor 2</article-title><source>Mol Cell Biochem</source><year>1981</year><volume>35</volume><fpage>129</fpage><lpage>158</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">6113539</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b27"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Pedulla</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The archaeal eIF2 homologue: functional properties of an ancient translation initiation factor</article-title><source>Nucleic Acids Res</source><year>2005</year><volume>33</volume><fpage>1804</fpage><lpage>1812</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15788752</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b28"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Pestova</surname><given-names>TV</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The joining of ribosomal subunits in eukaryotes requires eIF5B</article-title><source>Nature</source><year>2000</year><volume>403</volume><fpage>332</fpage><lpage>335</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10659855</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b29"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Roll-Mecak</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>X-Ray structures of the universal translation initiation factor IF2/eIF5B: conformational changes on GDP and GTP binding</article-title><source>Cell</source><year>2000</year><volume>103</volume><fpage>781</fpage><lpage>792</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11114334</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b30"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Roll-Mecak</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Engaging the ribosome: universal IFs of translation</article-title><source>Trends Biochem Sci</source><year>2001</year><volume>26</volume><fpage>705</fpage><lpage>709</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11738593</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b31"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Sartorius-Neef</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name></person-group><article-title><italic>In vivo</italic> studies on putative Shine&#x02013;Dalgarno sequences of the halophilic archaeon halobacterium salinarum</article-title><source>Mol Microbiol</source><year>2004</year><volume>51</volume><fpage>579</fpage><lpage>588</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14756795</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b32"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>She</surname><given-names>Q</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>The complete genome of the crenarchaeon <italic>Sulfolobus solfataricus</italic> P2</article-title><source>Proc Natl Acad Sci USA</source><year>2001</year><volume>98</volume><fpage>7835</fpage><lpage>7840</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11427726</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b33"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Shin</surname><given-names>BS</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Uncoupling of initiation factor eIF5B/IF2 GTPase and translational activities by mutations that lower ribosome affinity</article-title><source>Cell</source><year>2002</year><volume>111</volume><fpage>1015</fpage><lpage>1025</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12507428</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b34"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Spurio</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>The C-terminal subdomain (IF2 C-2) contains the entire fMet-tRNA binding site of initiation factor IF2</article-title><source>J Biol Chem</source><year>2000</year><volume>275</volume><fpage>2447</fpage><lpage>2454</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10644698</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b35"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Yatime</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Functional molecular mapping of archaeal translation initiation factor 2</article-title><source>J Biol Chem</source><year>2004</year><volume>279</volume><fpage>15984</fpage><lpage>15993</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14761973</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list></back></article>